                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lu8N0XCAsrg\n\nI had a lot of fun on Lightbender and there are a lot of puzzles ranging from very easy to very difficult. You have to get the light to change to the correct point whilst changing colours if necessary. There are also a lot of rules behind the game, as to what changes colours and what mirrors can move. In fact it’s probably wise to have a notepad with what happens to which colours and what makes it change to what colour. This can make the game a little scary. I just played the game blind and got pretty far and enjoyed what I played. I’m not the smartest of people though so I did get stuck quite often. Here are the positives and negatives:\n\nPositives :\n+ Great audio\n+ lots of content\n+ really good price\n+ Level editor\n+ Graphics are really good with the lighting effects\n+ Gamepad Support\n\nNegatives:\n- No Audio sliders\n- No Windowed Support\n- No Mouse Support\n - Lots of rules to colour changing which can be hard to memorise\n\nChanges:\n- No Audio sliders  (Audio sliders have been added 20th December)\n\nI personally would recommend this to people who enjoy puzzle games and those who enjoy light and colour changing. The music really adds to the game and I often find myself nodding and tapping my foot whilst trying to find out a solution. If you are slightly leaning towards purchasing, I would go and purchase it myself as for the price you really cannot lose (much.)\n\nI would not recommend if you are not very good at mixing colours or if you dislike keeping lots of notes down about colours. I would also not recommend to people who want to use their mouse as the game has no mouse support but keyboard and gamepad.\n\nOverall, I feel for the price it is worth a purchase.",
